<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.14.2: Using Tables to Compare Linear and Exponential Growth Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01499</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.14.2: Using Tables to Compare Linear and Exponential Growth Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9336ef50-c627-43f7-8f9d-5122d9586375</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title> Activity </title>
      <para id="para-00001">A family has $1,000 to invest and is considering two options: investing in government bonds that offer 2% simple
  interest, or investing in a savings account at a bank, which charges a $20 fee to open an account and pays 2% compound
  interest. Both options pay interest annually.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Here are two tables showing what they would earn in the first couple of years if they do not invest additional
  amounts or withdraw any money.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
              <entry>
               <emphasis>
          years of investment
       </emphasis> 
              </entry>
              <entry>
               <emphasis>
          amount in dollars
       </emphasis> 
              </entry>
            </row>
          <row>
              <entry>
          0
        </entry>
              <entry>
          $1,000
        </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
          1
        </entry>
              <entry>
          $1,020
        </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
          2
        </entry>
              <entry>
          $1,040
        </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody></tgroup><caption>Bonds</caption>
          </table>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00002">
          <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
              <entry>
               <emphasis>
          years of investment

       </emphasis> 
              </entry>
              <entry>
               <emphasis>
          amount in dollars
       </emphasis> 
              </entry>
            </row>
          <row>
              <entry>
          0
        </entry>
              <entry>
          $980
        </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
          1
        </entry>
              <entry>
          $999.60
        </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
          2
        </entry>
              <entry>
          $1,019.59
        </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody></tgroup><caption>Savings Account</caption>
          </table>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Bonds: How does the investment grow with simple interest?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">The investment grows linearly. It increases by $20 every year. 
  </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Savings account: How are the amounts $999.60 and $1,019.59 calculated?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">$999.60 is 1.02 times $980, and $1,019.59 is 1.02 times $999.60. 
  </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Write an equation to represent the relationship between the amount of money and the number of years of
        investment for each option.</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Write an equation for the data in the Bonds table. Use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent, if
        needed.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00012"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the years since the investments were made and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the value of the
      bonds.
  </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Write an equation for the data in the Savings Account table. Use the “^” symbol to enter an
        exponent, if needed.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00015"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>980</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.02</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the value of the savings account, in dollars, where and
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the years since the investments were made
  </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Which investment option should the family choose? Use your equations or calculations to support your answer.
    </para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">The family should choose bonds if they are investing for less than 12 years. After 12 years, the savings account
      contains $1,242.88 because this is the value from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>980</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.02</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. And, the bonds are worth $1,240
      after 12 years because <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is the value.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">The family should choose the savings account if they are saving for something long term like college or
      retirement. This is because over time, the savings account will contain more money than the bonds have earned. For
      instance, after 20 years, the savings account holds $1,456.23 from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">[</m:mo><m:mn>980</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.02</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">]</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> while the bonds
      only yield $1,400 from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">[</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">]</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool to graph the two investment options and show how the money grows in each.<media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/></media></item>
      </list>
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00020">Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00022">
                      <media alt=" GRAPH OF TWO FUNCTIONS, REPRESENTING TWO INVESTMENT OPTIONS. FUNCTION B IS LINEAR AND REPRESENTS THE BOND INVESTMENT. FUNCTION S IS EXPONENTIAL AND REPRESENTS THE SAVINGS INVESTMENT. THE X-AXIS IS LABELED YEARS OF INVESTMENT AND GOES FROM 0 TO 18. THE Y-AXIS IS LABELED AMOUNT IN DOLLARS AND GOES FROM 0 TO THIRTEEN HUNDRED. STARTING AT X EQUALS 12, THE EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION, S, SURPASSES THE LINEAR FUNCTION, B. ">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_16_03_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Using Tables to Compare Linear and Exponential Functions</title>
      <para id="para-00023">Watch the following video to learn more about comparing linear and exponential functions:</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b2c38efd9bdabdfa5993319c5eb1d37b24042ed2"/>
      </media>
    </section>

  <note class="self-check" id="note-00001"><para id="para-00024">Using a calculator, Joanna made the following table and then made the following conjecture: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is always greater than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.02</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. Is Joanna correct? Explain.</para>
